---
import Guide from "../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Theming</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    Colors, density, spacing, corners, fonts, elevation, motion and contrast.
    Each is a few custom properties on <code>html</code>, and a few classes
    change them for one part of the page.
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <h2 id="palette">Palette</h2>
    <p>
      The basic idea is to pick one hue and chroma, and derive a 16-step palette
      from them. <code>theme.css</code> sets <code>--ui-palette-hue</code> and <code
        >--ui-palette-chroma</code
      >, and <code>core/palette.css</code> turns them into a source color, <code
        >--ui-palette-source</code
      >, and the <code>--ui-color-1</code> to <code>--ui-color-16</code> steps.
    </p>

    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-palette-chroma: 0.5;
  --ui-palette-hue: var(--hue-green);
  --ui-palette-hue-rotate-by: 0;
}

:where(html.ui-dark) {
  --ui-palette-hue: var(--hue-blue);
}`}
      lang="css"
    />

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><code>--ui-palette-hue</code></strong> is a hue angle in degrees.
        Open Props' <code>--hue-*</code> tokens work here. The default is green in
        light mode and blue in dark mode.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>--ui-palette-chroma</code></strong> scales the saturation, from
        {" "}<code>0</code> (gray) to <code>1</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>--ui-palette-hue-rotate-by</code></strong> shifts the hue a
        little more on each step, in degrees. Step 2 turns by the value once, step
        3 twice, and so on, so <code>1</code> spreads 15° from <code
          >--ui-color-1</code
        > to
        <code>--ui-color-16</code>. The severity scopes use <code>1</code> for livelier
        tints, the brand palette <code>0</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      You can also set <code>--ui-palette-source</code> directly. Any color works:
      the palette keeps its oklch hue, scales its chroma and sets the lightness per
      step. Set it on <code>:root</code>, or on an element with <code
        >.ui-palette</code
      > to re-theme that part of the page. Anywhere else it has no effect: the palette
      is computed where <code>core/palette.css</code> declares it and only inherited
      from there. The same goes for <code>--ui-palette-hue</code>, <code
        >--ui-palette-chroma</code
      > and <code>--ui-palette-hue-rotate-by</code>.
    </p>

    <p>This is how the severity classes get their colors:</p>

    <Code
      code={`:where(.ui-warning) {
  --ui-palette-source: oklch(0.58 0.21 var(--hue-orange));
}`}
      lang="css"
    />

    <p>
      Every token with its default is listed on the <DocLink
        href="/guide/theme-tokens">Theme tokens</DocLink
      > page, and the <DocLink href="/guide/theme-generator"
        >Theme generator</DocLink
      > writes a complete <code>theme.css</code> for you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="scopes">Scopes</h2>
    <p>
      A scope is a class that changes the theme for one element and everything
      inside it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-light</code> and <code>.ui-dark</code> force a color scheme. Put
        them on <code>html</code> for the whole page, or on any element for just that
        part.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-critical</code>, <code>.ui-info</code>, <code
          >.ui-success</code
        >, <code>.ui-warning</code> and <code>.ui-neutral</code> swap the palette
        to red, blue, green, orange or gray. The <code>--ui-color-1</code> to <code
          >--ui-color-16</code
        > steps inside follow, while <code>--ui-primary</code> and the text colors
        keep the page values.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-palette</code> re-derives the palette and every color token from
        the knobs set on that element, so one page can carry several brands.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On an element, <code>.ui-dark</code> and <code>.ui-light</code> only switch
      between light and dark colors. The palette hue and the shadows still come from
      the page. So on a light page, a <code>.ui-dark</code> box stays green and keeps
      the light shadows, while a dark page is blue. Add <code>.ui-palette</code> with
      the dark hue to make the box match dark mode:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`<aside class="ui-dark ui-palette" style="--ui-palette-hue: var(--hue-blue)">
  Always dark
</aside>

<section class="ui-palette" style="--ui-palette-hue: 30">
  <button class="ui-button ui-primary ui-filled" type="button">
    Orange brand
  </button>
</section>`}
      lang="html"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="density">Density</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--ui-density</code> scales the <code>--ui-control-size*</code> tokens,
      so fields, buttons and list items grow or shrink together. Field and button
      padding shrinks to fit, down to the height of the text. The sizes resolve on
      {" "}<code>html</code>, so set <code>--ui-density</code> there.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-density: 0.875;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="spacing">Spacing</h2>
    <p>
      Containers space their content with the <code>--ui-space-1</code> to <code
        >--ui-space-8</code
      > steps: the padding of cards, callouts, dialogs, drawers, toasts and table
      cells, the gaps in forms, and the margins in rich text. Each step is the Open
      Props size of the same number times <code>--ui-space-scale</code>, so one
      value makes the whole page roomier or tighter. Controls keep following <code
        >--ui-density</code
      >, so a form can be spacious while its fields stay compact.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-space-scale: 1.25;
}

/* Or tie spacing to density */
:where(html) {
  --ui-density: 0.875;
  --ui-space-scale: var(--ui-density);
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      Like the control sizes, the steps resolve on <code>html</code>, so set <code
        >--ui-space-scale</code
      > there.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="corners">Corners</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--ui-border-radius</code> rounds every component. Cards, callouts, menus
      and dialogs read it directly, and buttons and fields follow it until you set
      <code>--ui-button-border-radius</code> or <code
        >--ui-field-border-radius</code
      >. Small parts, such as <code>kbd</code>, tooltips and checkboxes, keep
      their smaller radius but never get rounder than <code
        >--ui-border-radius</code
      >, so <code>0</code> squares everything. It works on a part of the page too.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-border-radius: 0;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fonts">Fonts</h2>
    <p>
      <code>--ui-font-body</code> sets the text, fields and buttons, <code
        >--ui-font-heading</code
      > the headings and the <code>.ui-h1</code> to <code>.ui-h6</code> classes, and
      <code>--ui-font-code</code> code, <code>kbd</code> and <code>samp</code>.
      Headings follow the body font until you set their own, also where you set <code
        >--ui-font-body</code
      > on a part of the page.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-font-body: "Inter", var(--font-sans);
  --ui-font-code: "JetBrains Mono", var(--font-mono);
  --ui-font-heading: "Fraunces", var(--font-serif);
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="elevation">Elevation</h2>
    <p>
      Two shadows lift things off the page. <code>--ui-shadow-raised</code> is for
      surfaces in the page, like elevated cards and accordions. It switches between
      <code>--ui-shadow-raised-light</code> and <code
        >--ui-shadow-raised-dark</code
      > with the color scheme, also in <code>.ui-light</code> and <code
        >.ui-dark</code
      > parts of the page, so set those two. <code>--ui-shadow-overlay</code> is for
      layers above the page: menus, <code>select</code> pickers, drawers and toasts.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-shadow-overlay: var(--shadow-4);
  --ui-shadow-raised-dark: none;
  --ui-shadow-raised-light: none;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="motion">Motion</h2>
    <p>
      Use the <code>--ui-motion</code> variable to turn motion on or off. The default
      value is <code>1</code>. If a user has <code
        >prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code
      > enabled,
      {" "}<code>--ui-motion</code> will be set to <code>0</code> by default.
    </p>

    <h3 id="motion-global">Global classes</h3>
    <p>
      Adding these utility classes to the <code>html</code> element will override
      the OS preference.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-motion-off</code>: sets <code>--ui-motion: 0</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-motion-on</code>: sets <code>--ui-motion: 1</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-motion-debug</code>: sets <code>--ui-motion: 10</code> (slows down
        transitions 10x).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code code={`<html lang="en" class="ui-motion-debug">`} lang="html" />

    <h3 id="motion-local">Local overrides</h3>
    <p>
      Components use a local <code>--_motion</code> variable that allows you to disable
      motion for each component individually if you want.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`<button class="ui-button" style="--_motion: 0" type="button">
  Instant interaction
</button>`}
      lang="html"
    />

    <p>
      Additionally, this is how you could include <code>--ui-motion</code> in your
      CSS:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`transition: transform calc(var(--ui-duration) * var(--ui-motion)) var(--ui-ease);`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="contrast">Contrast</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>--ui-contrast</code> variable is <code>normal</code> by default. If
      a user has <code>prefers-contrast: more</code> enabled, it is set to <code
        >more</code
      >, and a style query raises the contrast of muted text, borders, field
      borders, the primary color, intent colors and the focus ring. Components
      with translucent text, such as Tabs, keyboard hints and inline code,
      follow along.
    </p>
    <p>
      Try it with the <strong>High contrast</strong> switch in the theme config drawer.
    </p>

    <Example row showCode={false}>
      <div class="contrast-demo">
        <section>
          <p class="ui-caption">Default</p>
          <label class="ui-text-field">
            <span class="ui-label">Email</span>
            <span class="ui-field">
              <input type="email" placeholder="name@example.com" />
            </span>
            <span class="ui-end-text">We'll never share it.</span>
          </label>
          <button class="ui-button ui-primary ui-outlined" type="button">
            Subscribe
          </button>
        </section>
        <section class="ui-contrast-more">
          <p class="ui-caption">.ui-contrast-more</p>
          <label class="ui-text-field">
            <span class="ui-label">Email</span>
            <span class="ui-field">
              <input type="email" placeholder="name@example.com" />
            </span>
            <span class="ui-end-text">We'll never share it.</span>
          </label>
          <button class="ui-button ui-primary ui-outlined" type="button">
            Subscribe
          </button>
        </section>
      </div>
    </Example>

    <h3 id="contrast-classes">Classes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-contrast-more</code>: sets <code>--ui-contrast: more</code>.
        Put it on{" "}
        <code>html</code> for the whole page, or on any element to raise the contrast
        of its children.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>.ui-contrast-normal</code>: sets <code>--ui-contrast: normal</code
        >. Put it on <code>html</code> to ignore the OS preference. It doesn't lower
        the contrast inside a <code>.ui-contrast-more</code> ancestor, since the tokens
        are already resolved on the parent.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code code={`<html lang="en" class="ui-contrast-more">`} lang="html" />

    <h3 id="contrast-custom">Custom values</h3>
    <p>
      Style queries match against the parent element, so the high-contrast
      values are set on <code>body</code> instead of <code>html</code>. They are
      set again on the children of <code>.ui-contrast-more</code> and on the surfaces
      that have their own border colors: <code>.ui-palette</code>, elevated and
      tonal accordions and cards, default callouts, filled lists and table
      headers. They replace any value you set on
      {" "}<code>html</code> for the same tokens. To tune them, write your own style
      query with the same selectors:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@container style(--ui-contrast: more) {
  :where(
    body,
    .ui-contrast-more > *,
    .ui-palette,
    .ui-accordion:is(.ui-elevated, .ui-tonal),
    .ui-callout:not(
      .ui-critical,
      .ui-info,
      .ui-neutral,
      .ui-outlined,
      .ui-success,
      .ui-warning
    ),
    .ui-card:is(.ui-elevated, .ui-tonal),
    .ui-list:not(.ui-tonal, .ui-transparent),
    .ui-table th
  ) {
    --ui-border-color: var(--ui-text-muted);
    --ui-field-border-color: var(--ui-border-color);
  }
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      Tokens that reference an overridden token, like <code
        >--ui-field-border-color</code
      > above, have to be set again in the same rule.
    </p>

    <h3 id="forced-colors">Forced colors</h3>
    <p>
      When an OS contrast theme forces its own palette (<code
        >forced-colors: active</code
      >), components switch to system colors so their state stays visible.
      Selected tabs, toggles and list items use <code>SelectedItem</code>,
      switches, ranges, progress bars and dividers are drawn with <code
        >CanvasText</code
      >, and focused fields get a <code>Highlight</code> outline. There is nothing
      to configure.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>

<style>
  .contrast-demo {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--size-4);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
    inline-size: 100%;

    section {
      align-items: start;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: var(--size-3);
    }

    .ui-text-field {
      inline-size: 100%;
    }
  }
</style>
